  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">UNDP Contribution for the Benefit of the IOM, to Strengthen the Collection and Processing of Data and the Operationalization of a Drought Early Warning System</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Due to its climatic, social and economic characteristics, Madagascar is certainly one of the countries most exposed and vulnerable to climatic hazards. The drought regularly affecting the south of the country is only one of their manifestations, but its importance requires joint action and solid tools to better anticipate, respond and recover. Early Warning Systems (EWS) are therefore essential elements making it possible to inform decisions to better anticipate, prepare, respond and recover from this type of crisis. Furthermore, the Anticipatory Actions (AA) which will be triggered by the alerts must be part of harmonized and concrete frameworks, validated by all stakeholders under the leadership of the government. It is with this observation that a consortium of four agencies (UNDP, WFP, UNICEF and FAO) prepared and won a two-year project financed by ECHO (European Civil Protection and Humanitarian Aid Operations) entitled &quot;Operationalization of the System of &quot;Early Warning (SAP) and Anticipatory Actions (AA) in the Great South for anticipation of the risk of drought and increased resilience of the populations and institutions of Madagascar&quot;.</narrative>
  </description>
